back in 2010 developer frictional games  set the tone for pc  in the horror with amnesia the dark  descent with amnesia rebirth  it feels like frictional has finally  refined its story-driven atmospheric  approach to horror  you're trapped in a deadly smothering  world struggling against your  character's limitations and even her  perceptions  rebirth is frictional's best game yet  marrying a deep mysterious story  to the signature mechanics the developer  has been working with over the course of  13 years of horror games  amnesia rebirth continues frictional  specific approach to story and horror  which emphasizes avoiding conflict  hiding and mastering your character's  own fear  it adds to the story of the dark descent  although you don't need to know that  game well to follow this narrative  and it doesn't connect to amnesia a  machine for pigs at all  you play as tassie trionen a french  woman who joins a mining expedition to  algeria in 1937.  in the opening moments of the game the  expedition's plane experiences some  supernatural shenanigans that cause it  to crash in the desert  tosi wakes up soon after alone in the  plane with few memories of what happened  and strange black tendrils creeping into  her vision  as she goes searching for survivors she  discovers that the strange bracelet  she's wearing can open portals to a dark  ruined parallel world tassie goes  searching through caves and tunnels for  her friends  and the story often pulls her into the  alternate dimension as she tries to find  her way forward  rebirth brings back the main mechanics  from the dark descent and really  all of frictional's games deal in  similar sets of ideas  you trace the survivor's path gathering  notes and uncovering clues as to what  happened  you're quickly plunged into darkness  which increases tassie's fear and  affects her psyche  itasi gets too scared the blackness  overtakes her causing her to wake up  somewhere else with no memory of how she  got there  the more frightened tassie becomes the  less reliable her perceptions  you start to see frightening flashes  across the screen as she becomes more  terrified  and rebirth plays off your inability to  see much around you with sound design  that emphasizes the echoing  oppressive nature of the places you work  through just as things are starting to  get spooky or dangerous  images start to flash in your vision to  create jump scares and the addition of  sound and movement in your periphery  makes it tougher to trust your senses  you're often hearing things that might  be lurking just outside of your vision  and rebirth increases your terror by  making it difficult to tell if those  things are hallucinations  ambient noise or actual dangers waiting  in the dark  to fight the fear you're always on the  hunt for light  finding books of matches all over the  environment you can use to push back the  darkness  matches quickly burn out but you can use  them to ignite torches  candles sconces and other light sources  as you explore  you'll spend a lot of your time lighting  every flammable thing you can find  but this has the drawback of leaving you  with fewer places to hide from whatever  is making those upsetting chewing sounds  somewhere beyond your vision  like frictional's other games you can  interact with most everything you see in  rebirth  using your mouse or controller to  simulate motions like pushing open doors  or moving objects  you use those physical interactions to  solve puzzles that block your progress  while none of the puzzles are especially  difficult it's definitely possible to  get stalled out as you search for a  solution that's not immediately  intuitive  for the most part though the obstacles  rebirth throws in your path are  inventive without being annoyed  while you're exploring you'll also have  to contend with actual terrors hiding in  the shadows  you can't fight the monsters skittering  in the dark you can only hide or flee  from them  that leads to a variety of frightening  chases and stealthy moments as you sneak  past something horrifying while it hunts  you  balancing using shadows to hide yourself  and keeping taci's fears from  overwhelming her  it's in these brushes with supernatural  horrors that rebirth represents the  biggest step forward for frictional  games  your only measure for how tassy is  faring is her increasing heart rate  and the black encroaching across her  vision as her fear rises  but if a creature catches you it doesn't  kill you forcing you to reload a  checkpoint and pretend nothing happened  instead if things get bad enough the  black tendrils overtake tassie  and you find yourself somewhere else it  might sound like the lack of deadly  enemies means it lacks stakes but the  game still does a great job  of creating frightening moments but  removes the frustration of having to  replay a section after dying  and it feels like a natural evolution  from frictionals past games  this is a game about a spooky story and  while horror games work to create  moments of fear and tension  a lot of what makes them scary gets  ruined by playing the same section of  the game over and over  storytelling is rebirth's real focus  although the intricate tale it tells is  undercut somewhat by the limitations of  small games and the trappings of first  person horror  most of the game is about tassie finding  her way alone reading notes and  remembering tidbits of stories that  happened off-screen  those flashbacks are largely presented  as dialogue over drawings  and that can make it tough to understand  or connect with the many characters  mentioned along the way  tasi's personal story which focuses on  her experiences as a mother  is a lot stronger her relationships with  her daughter and her husband  and the traumas of her past inform a lot  of her actions as rebirth progresses  although it's not until late in the game  that you really start to understand  tossy as a character  thanks to her amnesia rebirth is  unabashedly a mom game  and it's at its best when tassie's  personal journey intersects with the  supernatural  precious tune and look at you  though sneaking past or fleeing from  monsters is often harrowing there are  moments when encounters won't quite  terrify  the creatures sometimes seem a bit dumb  as you slip past them and one or two  chases move fairly slowly  making it apparent you're really not in  as much danger as the game would like  you to believe  the limitation that you can't actually  look at enemies because it drives up  tossy's fear  can also seem a bit arbitrary at times  with a few moments that wouldn't be very  scary or challenging  if you could actually see what you're  avoiding still amnesia rebirth feels  like the culmination of frictional games  horror titles up to this point  it refines the ideas and mechanics that  have been central to the developers  games all along  it hones in on effective scares while  avoiding the frustrations of failure  and it tightens the focus on  character-driven storytelling  amnesia rebirth is an unsettling strange  tragic story  that deepens the amnesia mythos in a lot  of cool ways  while managing to be just as creepy and  frightening as its beloved predecessor  [Music]  you
 

